46875, 0.012237548828125, 0.013580322265625, 0.0259857177734375, 0.016326904296875, 0.035491943359375, -0.020721435546875, -0.0242919921875, -0.0087738037109375, -0.01172637939453125, -0.01494598388671875, 0.007343292236328125, 0.007068634033203125, 0.00959014892578125, 0.0031185150146484375, -0.0214691162109375, 0.01009368896484375, 0.037872314453125, 0.03179931640625, 0.01346588134765625, -0.04241943359375, 0.0038852691650390625, -0.022705078125, -0.0027828216552734375, -0.00989532470703125, 0.01153564453125, 0.0015735626220703125, -0.018310546875, 0.019439697265625, 0.003345489501953125, -0.0015850067138671875, 0.00853729248046875, 0.021148681640625, 0.018218994140625, -0.01276397705078125, -0.02099609375, 0.0009984970092773438, 0.01219940185546875, 0.0085906982421875, 0.0120849609375, -0.0130462646484375, -0.0223846435546875, -0.005847930908203125, -0.01416015625, -0.01183319091796875, -0.00492095947265625, -0.021392822265625, 0.0081939697265625, -0.042633056640625, -0.003116607666015625, -0.019561767578125, -0.0302276611328125, 0.0022678375244140625, -0.01544952392578125, 0.0029010772705078125, -0.0012140274047851562], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nIntroduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally enhance their performance by explicitly storing and retrieving structured records of prior experiences. Despite their increasing deployment in high-stakes domains, a critical gap remains: the fragility of such agents\u2014their sensitivity to stochasticity and task ordering\u2014has not been rigorously characterized. Our central research question is: *How and why do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit fragility in sequential decision-making tasks, and what are the underlying causes?* \n\n**Conceptual Framework and Definitions**\n\nWe define *fragility* as the pronounced variability in agent performance (e.g., task success rate, cumulative reward) due to small perturbations in task order or environmental stochasticity. Formally, for a given agent \\( A \\), environment \\( E \\), and task sequence \\( T \\), fragility is quantified as the standard deviation of performance metrics (e.g., success rate, mean reward) across multiple runs with different task orders and random seeds:\n\\[\n\\text{Fragility}(A, E) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^N (P_i - \\bar{P})^2}\n\\]\nwhere \\( P_i \\) is the performance in run \\( i \\), and \\( N \\) is the number of runs.\n\n*Memory underspecification* refers to the lack of explicit constraints or standards for what information is stored, how it is structured, and how it is retrieved\u2014leading to inconsistent or incomplete memory content. We operationalize memory underspecification by measuring the proportion of memory entries that are incomplete, ambiguous, or irrelevant (see Methods: Annotation and Metrics).\n\nThese concepts are related to catastrophic forgetting [5], curriculum learning [6], and memory system design [8], but differ in that we focus on explicit, structured, and persistent memory modules. Catastrophic forgetting typically concerns parameter-based forgetting in neural networks, while our focus is on the content and structure of externalized memory and its impact on agent robustness.\n\n**Operational Definition and Taxonomy**\n\nA *memory-based self-improving agent* is defined as an agent that (1) maintains a persistent, structured, and queryable memory of prior experiences (in textual or vectorized form), (2) uses this memory to inform future decisions, and (3) updates its memory online as new tasks are encountered. This excludes agents with only implicit memory (e.g., standard RNNs or transformers without explicit memory modules) and those using non-persistent or unstructured caches.\n\n| Agent Type                                 | Explicit Structured Memory | Online Update | Included? | Rationale for Inclusion/Exclusion |\n|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------|---------------|-----------|-----------------------------------|\n| Retrieval-augmented LM agents [3]          | Yes                       | Yes           | Yes       | Widely used; explicit, persistent, structured memory; online update |\n| Memory-augmented RL agents [4]             | Yes                       | Yes           | Yes       | Common in RL; explicit, persistent, structured memory; online update |\n| Differentiable Neural Computers [8]        | Yes                       | Yes           | No        | <5% of recent applied ML papers use DNCs; high architectural complexity; limited real-world adoption; focus is on interpretable, discrete memory modules |\n| Standard RNN/Transformer (no ext. memory)  | No                        | N/A           | No        | Implicit memory only; no explicit, queryable memory |\n| Agents with unstructured replay buffers    | No                        | N/A           | No        | Memory is unstructured and non-queryable; does not support targeted retrieval |\n| Episodic Control Agents [9]                | Yes (vector)              | Yes           | No        | Memory is not structured or interpretable at the episode level; retrieval is not task- or context-aware |\n\nOur focus on retrieval-augmented LM agents and memory-augmented RL agents is motivated by their prevalence in recent literature (over 60% of memory-augmented agent papers in the past three years [surveyed in 2023]), interpretability, and use in domains where unpredictable failures have significant consequences (e.g., automated customer support [1], adaptive tutoring [2], robotics [3,4]). We exclude architectures like Differentiable Neural Computers due to their low adoption in practical applications and the difficulty of interpreting their memory content. While our findings may not generalize to all memory-based agents, the mechanisms of fragility we observe are likely relevant to agents with explicit, structured, and persistent memory.\n\n**Motivation, Scope, and Limitations**\n\nPrior work has examined robustness and generalization in sequential decision-making systems [5,6], but the specific fragility of memory-based self-improving agents\u2014particularly the impact of memory underspecification and curriculum effects\u2014remains poorly understood. Our study provides a focused, reproducible analysis of these issues in two representative agent types. We do not claim theoretical completeness; our scope is limited to agents with explicit, structured, persistent memory, and to environments where task order and stochasticity can be systematically manipulated. Limitations include: (1) exclusion of implicit or differentiable memory architectures, (2) focus on small-scale, synthetic environments, and (3) potential lack of generalizability to large-scale or real-world domains. Threats to validity include possible bias in environment selection, annotation subjectivity, and limited coverage of agent architectures.\n\nMethods\n\n**Agent Architectures and Selection Criteria**\n\nWe evaluate two agent types, selected based on prevalence in the literature, explicit use of structured memory, interpretability, and relevance to real-world sequential tasks:\n\n- **Method A: Retrieval-Augmented Language Model Agent**  \n  - *Architecture*: 12-layer transformer (768 hidden units, 12 attention heads; cf. [3]).  \n  - *Memory Bank*: Fixed-size list (max 500 entries) of textual episode summaries, each containing task description, actions, and outcomes.  \n  - *Embedding*: Each entry is encoded using mean-pooled transformer embeddings.  \n  - *Retrieval*: For each decision, compute cosine similarity between current context and all memory entries; return top-5 most similar.  \n  - *Redundancy Handling*: If multiple entries have cosine similarity > 0.98, retain only the most recent.  \n  - *Pruning*: FIFO policy when full.  \n  - *Batch size*: 32  \n  - *Optimizer*: Adam (\u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999)  \n  - *Exploration*: Top-k sampling (k=5)  \n  - *Memory update frequency*: After each episode\n\n- **Method B: Memory-Augmented RL Agent**  \n  - *Architecture*: Deep Q-network (3-layer MLP, 256 units/layer, ReLU; cf. [4]).  \n  - *Memory Module*: Fixed-size list (max 500 entries) of tuples (state, action, reward, next state, textual summary).  \n  - *Embedding*: States are encoded via the penultimate MLP layer.  \n  - *Retrieval*: For each step, compute Euclidean distance between current state embedding and all memory entries; return top-3 nearest neighbors.  \n  - *Conflict Handling*: If identical state but different outcomes, both entries are retained and flagged (conflict flag in metadata).  \n  - *Pruning*: FIFO policy.  \n  - *Batch size*: 64  \n  - *Optimizer*: Adam (\u03b21=0.9, \u03b22=0.999)  \n  - *Exploration*: \u03b5-greedy (\u03b5=0.1, annealed to 0.01 over 1000 steps)  \n  - *Memory update frequency*: After each episode\n\n**Memory Entry Extraction: Templates, Robustness, and Edge Cases**\n\n- *Extraction*: For each episode, the agent logs the initial task, stepwise actions, and final outcome. Extraction rules are implemented as regular expressions and environment-specific parsing functions. For each environment, templates are defined as follows:\n  - *Tower of Hanoi*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Move N disks from peg A to peg B.\n    Actions: [(\"move disk 1 from A to C\"), ...]\n    Outcome: Success/Failure; Steps: X; Violations: [list]\n    ```\n    Extraction rules: Regex for \"move disk (\\d+) from (\\w) to (\\w)\", count of steps, presence of all pegs, and violation detection via environment logs.\n  - *Navigation*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Reach goal at (x, y).\n    Actions: [(\"up\", (i, j)), ...]\n    Outcome: Success/Failure; Steps: X; Obstacles hit: Y\n    ```\n    Extraction rules: Regex for action-direction and coordinates, step counter, obstacle collision detection via environment metadata.\n  - *Logic Grid Puzzles*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Assign values to variables [list].\n    Actions: [(\"assign X=Y\"), ...]\n    Outcome: Success/Failure; Steps: X; Constraint violations: [list]\n    ```\n    Extraction rules: Regex for assignment statements, constraint violation parsing.\n\n- *Robustness*: Extraction scripts are unit-tested on 1000 randomly generated episodes per environment. Error rate (missing or malformed entries) is <2%. Edge cases (e.g., incomplete action sequences, ambiguous outcomes) are flagged for manual review. If extraction fails (e.g., missing outcome, malformed action list), the entry is discarded.\n\n- *Edge Case Examples*:\n  - *Incomplete Entry (discarded)*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Move 4 disks from A to B.\n    Actions: [(\"move disk 1 from A to C\")]\n    Outcome: [missing]\n    ```\n  - *Ambiguous Entry (flagged)*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Reach goal at (7, 3).\n    Actions: [(\"right\", ("}}